{
  "title": "Check the count and compare names",
  "text": "The assignment requires exactly two arguments: [ \"$#\" -ne 2 ] succeeds when the supplied argument count is not two. -ne compares numbers; [ \"$input\" = \"$output\" ] compares text exactly. A || B tries B only if A fails; a guard joining invalid checks with || rejects when any check succeeds. The small example has no supplied arguments, so it creates count=2 and two filenames to practice these tests. In the assignment, use the real \"$#\", \"$1\" and \"$2\", and reject an empty output with -z, before any > or >>."

},
{
  "title": "Require enough arguments before preflight",
  "text": "\"$#\" is the number of supplied arguments, including empty ones. [ \"$#\" -lt 2 ] succeeds when that number is less than two; -lt compares whole numbers, unlike -z, which tests empty text. A destination plus at least one source needs two arguments. The example also receives two arguments, both source names, so its new guard lets the existing file check run. Guard the count before saving $1 and shifting in the assignment."

},
{
  "title": "Combine the file and filename checks",
  "text": "A && B runs B only when A succeeds with status 0. In the example, [ -f \"$item\" ] first checks that the selected name is a real virtual file; [ \"$item\" = \"$target\" ] then tests whether its text exactly equals the destination name. Both checks must succeed to reject a collision. The = inside [ ] compares two values; target=practice.txt outside [ ] assigns a value."

},
{
  "title": "Recognize the test command spelling",
  "text": "test -z \"$2\" is the same empty-text check as [ -z \"$2\" ]. With test, omit the closing ]; the options and success statuses are the same. test \"$#\" -ne 2 checks whether the supplied argument count differs from two. As in earlier guards, A || B tries B only after A fails, so either invalid check can select the error branch. The example uses this spelling before its new case decision."

original_teaching.example:
if test "$#" -ne 2 || test -z "$2"; then
    printf 'Need VIEW TITLE\n' >&2
    exit 2
fi
case "$1" in
    quick|summary) printf 'Loan %s: %s\n' "$2" "$NOTE" ;;
    detail) printf 'Loan %s\n' "$2"; cat museum.txt ;;
    *) printf 'Unknown view\n' >&2; exit 2 ;;
esac

